Ladybird keeps the character geometry of each text node from one
build of the accessibility tree to the next. On every build, it checks
that geometry against the line content layout kept and that content’s
position, and computes it again when either changed. The other engines
have layout notify accessibility instead. WebKit’s RenderBlockFlow calls
AXObjectCache::onTextRunsChanged(), which queues an AXProperty::TextRuns
update for the node in its AXIsolatedTree. Blink’s LayoutText calls
AXObjectCacheImpl::InlineTextBoxesUpdated() when its inline text boxes
change, which marks the object dirty. Gecko keeps text geometry in its
CacheDomain::TextBounds domain, which any Text or Bounds update brings
along (GetCacheDomainsQueueUpdateSuperset() in DocAccessible). Ladybird
checks rather than gets notified, since its layout doesn’t report which
boxes changed to the code that builds the tree.
